page
title Chapter One: The Ferry at Dusk
gap 18
column
para
| The ferry left the north bank an hour before
| dark, and the ferryman lit his lantern before
| the boat had cleared the reeds. He did it every
| evening, whether or not anyone was waiting on
| the far side.
para
| Most nights the crossing took twenty minutes.
| The river was wide there and slow, and the
| current pulled the boat a little downstream, so
| that he had to aim for the old willow and let
| the water carry him to the landing. Passengers
| who had made the crossing before liked to
| watch him do it, and the ones who had not
| always asked why he steered for the tree and
| not the
footer 36 1
page
header 10pt 2 · A Lantern for the Ones Who Walk at Night
column
| dock, until he showed them where the current
| would leave them if he did.
para
| He kept a ledger of every crossing in a tin box
| under the bench. It held the date, the weather,
| the number of passengers, and a line or two
| about each of them, written small so that one
| page would last a week. Nobody had asked him to
| keep it. He said that a river forgets everyone
| who crosses it, and that somebody ought to
| remember them for a while.
para
| On the evening this story begins, there was one
| passenger on the south bank: a girl of about
| twelve with a suitcase tied shut with string and
| a letter she held in both hands, as if the wind
| might
page
header 10pt What the Ferryman Kept in the Hold of His Boat · 3
column
| take it from her. She asked whether the boat
| went as far as the mill. He told her that it went
| only across, and that the mill was a long walk
| from the landing, but that he would show her the
| path.
para
| She sat at the front of the boat with the
| suitcase on her knees. Halfway over she asked
| what the lantern was for, since the sky was still
| light enough to see the far bank. He said that it
| was not for him.
para
| It was for anyone on the water who had lost the
| landing, and for anyone on the bank who was
| wondering whether the ferry was still running.
page
header 10pt 4 · A Lantern for the Ones Who Walk at Night
column
para
| The girl thought about that for the rest of the
| crossing. When they reached the willow she
| watched the current take the boat, just as he
| had said it would, and she laughed when it
| bumped against the landing as gently as a cat
| against a door.
para
| He tied the boat up and walked with her as far
| as the first bend in the path. There he gave her
| the lantern, because the walk to the mill went
| through the wood and would be dark long before
| she
page
header 10pt What the Ferryman Kept in the Hold of His Boat · 5
column
| reached it. She promised to bring it back in the
| morning.
para
| That night he crossed the river without a light
| for the first time in eleven years. He wrote the
| crossing in the ledger by the glow of the stove,
| and under it, in letters a little larger than
| usual, he wrote that the lantern was out on loan.
